{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And it shall be when your son asks you</b>, etc. Meaning: at the time your son sees the matter of redemption of the firstborn, if he asks you, you are obligated to tell him; but without a question there is no obligation except on the night of Pesach. Therefore the verse is precise in saying <b>tomorrow</b> — meaning, even tomorrow, that is, at any time that may be, and it says <b>to say</b> — meaning, that his intention in the question should be that you will say to him; then is when you answer him in the manner stated. But if he says ״What is this?״ in a manner of disrespect, not with the intention of seeking an answer — you shall not answer him thus." "<b>Further</b>, it intends that the question need not be in this exact formulation, ״What is this?״, but rather even if it is in whatever manner it may be, so long as the intent of the question is ״to say, What is this?״ We thus conclude that this verse was not stated regarding the night of Pesach, but rather regarding the commandment of the sanctity of the firstborn of the womb, and there is no need for the telling except by means of a question. And our Sages of blessed memory (Mekhilta) said: corresponding to four sons, etc. — this is the way of midrash."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:28.525539826Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}